# ai.wing — Bluepeak Authorized Retailer

Changelog and architecture record for this site. Every significant structural
change, feature addition and architectural decision gets an entry here so work
can be tracked and reverted cleanly.

Newest entries at the bottom.

---

## [0.1.0] Initial build — scaffold and toolchain

**Stack**
- Next.js 15.5.25 (App Router) + React 19 + TypeScript 5.7 (strict)
- Tailwind CSS 3.4 with a hand-built token layer
- framer-motion 11.18 for animation, lenis 1.1 for smooth scroll
- No backend, no database, no API routes. Strict front-end build.

**Decisions**
- Scaffolded by hand rather than with `create-next-app`. The project directory
  name contains a space, which `create-next-app` rejects as an npm package
  name.
- Pinned Next to **15.5.25** rather than 15.1.6. The 15.1.x line carries
  CVE-2025-66478; 15.5.25 is the patched release on the `backport` tag. Stayed
  on the 15.x line rather than jumping to 16.x to avoid a major-version
  migration mid-build.
- ESLint is not installed and `.eslintrc.json` was removed. `tsc --noEmit` is
  the type gate; `next build` is the correctness gate.

---

## [0.2.0] Brand extraction from mybluepeak.com

`mybluepeak.com` returns 403 to the fetch proxies and to the in-app browser, but
serves normal HTML to `curl` with a browser User-Agent. It also rate-limits
after roughly one request, so pages were pulled one at a time.

**Palette** — lifted verbatim from the site's published theme tokens
(`--wp--preset--color--*`), not eyeballed from screenshots:

| Token | Hex | Use here |
|---|---|---|
| navy-blue | `#060048` | dominant brand navy, dark sections, header CTA |
| dark-blue | `#0a109d` | royal accent, gradients |
| blue | `#034fab` | mid blue, gradients |
| teal | `#008dbb` | **primary CTA and accent** |
| yellow | `#ebab32` | energetic accent |
| light-gray | `#f3f3ed` | warm off-white section background |
| gray | `#e7e6dc` | chips, hairlines |
| white | `#FFFFFF` | cards, body |

**Typography** — Bluepeak uses **Sharp Sans**, which is commercially licensed
and not redistributable. Substituted **Figtree** (Google Fonts, self-hosted via
`next/font`), the closest open match in weight, width and geometry.

**Logo** — deliberately an original mark (three ascending strands forming a
peak), not a reproduction of Bluepeak's own logo. "Authorized Retailer" is
locked to the wordmark wherever it appears.

---

## [0.3.0] Service-line audit — what Bluepeak actually sells

Audited the residential navigation on mybluepeak.com. "For Home" contains
exactly: Fiber Internet, Whole-Home Wi-Fi, Moving, TV.

| Line | Verdict | Reasoning |
|---|---|---|
| **Fiber** | INCLUDED | 1 Gig / 2 Gig / 5 Gig, symmetrical |
| **Cable** | OMITTED | No separately sold residential cable tier; fiber-to-the-home is the residential product |
| **Bundles** | INCLUDED | Internet + TV is genuinely sold; no published bundle price |
| **TV** | INCLUDED | My Locals / + Favorites / + More, plus Bluepeak Stream |
| **Mobile** | OMITTED | Bluepeak sells no mobile or wireless plan |
| **Phone** | OMITTED | Hosted Voice is a **business** product; no residential phone offering |

Third-party comparison sites (cabletv.com, compareinternet.com) claim Bluepeak
sells no TV at all. That is wrong — `mybluepeak.com/tv/` lists three live
packages. Official site wins over aggregators on every disputed fact.

Omitted lines are removed entirely rather than stubbed. No "call to ask" cards,
no empty placeholders.

**Verified plan data**
- 1 Gig — $55/mo, 1-year price lock
- 2 Gig — $75/mo, 2-year price lock
- 5 Gig — $100/mo, 5-year price lock
- All: symmetrical, modem + eero included, unlimited data, no annual contract,
  free installation when ordered online
- Autopay required for advertised rates: $10/mo bank (ACH), $5/mo credit card
- Add-ons: Service Protection +$3/mo, eero Plus +$9/mo, Advanced Wi-Fi +$15/mo
- TV: My Locals 14+, + Favorites 78+, + Favorites + More 136+ — **no published
  pricing**, which is why those cards read "Call for pricing"
- Footprint: ND, SD, MN, OK, TX, WY — 31 named communities

Two figures circulating on aggregator sites were **deliberately excluded**
because they could not be confirmed on the official site: a $2.70/mo network
maintenance fee, and $70/$115 unreturned-equipment fees. Unverified numbers do
not belong in a pricing disclosure.

---

## [0.4.0] Centralised data architecture — `lib/content.ts`

Single source of truth. Nothing about a plan is written in a `.tsx` file.

**Core model** — `PlanItem` carries `id`, `name`, `serviceLine`, `speedDown`,
`speedUp`, `price`, `cents`, `promoQualifier`, `equipmentFee`, `dataPolicy`,
`contractTerm`, `features`, `isPopular`, plus optional presentation extras
(`tagline`, `bestFor`, `channelCount`, `priceLock`, `badge`).

**The cascade layer** — derived exports, not duplicated data:

- `plansByLine(line)` — plans for one service line
- `activeServiceLines` — canonical order, filtered to lines that *have* plans.
  This is the mechanism that removes Cable, Mobile and Phone from the page and
  from the nav simultaneously. Add a plan with `serviceLine: 'phone'` and a
  Phone section appears in the right position with no layout edit.
- `heroPlan` — cheapest priced plan, drives the hero anchor
- `topSpeedGbps` — computed from the lineup, used in the hero subline, the
  metadata description and the FAQ
- `navLinks` / `footerColumns` — generated from `activeServiceLines`
- `finePrint.columns` / `.rows` — the entire comparison table is generated from
  the fiber plans, so it cannot drift from the cards
- `legal.disclosures` — the pricing disclosure string is assembled from the
  actual plan prices
- `ctaLabelFor(plan)` — the single rule deciding CTA text

**To change a price:** edit the `price` field in `lib/content.ts`. It updates
the hero lockup, the plan card, the comparison table and the footer legal
disclosure at once. Nothing else needs touching.

---

## [0.5.0] `<PriceLockup />` — the universal price renderer

One component renders every price on the site: hero anchor, plan cards,
hero lineup list. Visual flex row of `$` + dominant integer + muted cents +
muted `/mo`.

- Dominant integer held inside the 2.5rem–3.5rem band at all breakpoints via
  `clamp()` across three sizes (`hero`, `card`, `compact`)
- `$` and cents are baseline-offset against the integer, not centred
- A plan with no `price` renders a "Call for pricing" lockup instead — this is
  what the TV and bundle cards use
- Screen readers get the figure as one `sr-only` sentence rather than
  fragmented digits

---

## [0.6.0] Animation system

`SiteMotion` wraps the app: Lenis smooth scroll plus framer-motion's
`LazyMotion` with the `domAnimation` bundle only. Everything animated is an
`m.*` element rather than `motion.*`, which keeps roughly 25kb of gzipped JS off
the critical path.

| Effect | Where | Implementation |
|---|---|---|
| Lenis smooth scroll | global | dynamic `import('lenis')`, rAF loop, anchor clicks routed through `lenis.scrollTo` |
| Black-hole / fiber field | hero | canvas; strands spiral into a core, accelerating and streaking as they fall |
| Scroll-scrubbed assembly | how-it-works | `NetworkAssembly` — fiber, wall terminal, modem, eero and devices assemble strictly as a function of scroll |
| SVG path draw | same | `pathLength` scrubbed off `scrollYProgress` |
| Travelling data pulse | same | CSS `stroke-dashoffset` keyframe, zero JS |
| Scroll-triggered text reveal | headings | `WordReveal`, per-word mask reveal |
| Staggered grid entrance | all card grids | `StaggerGroup` / `StaggerItem` variants |
| Parallax depth | hero, coverage | `useScroll` + `useTransform` |
| Clip-path wipe | section boundaries | `ClipWipe`, slanted top edge levels out on scroll |
| 3D card tilt | plan cards | `TiltCard`, pointer-tracked rotate + specular glare |
| Magnetic buttons | CTAs | `MagneticButton`, spring-follows-cursor |
| Infinite marquee | hero ticker | pure CSS, duplicated track translated -50% |

**Performance guards**
- Canvas: DPR capped at 1.5, strand count scales with viewport, rAF loop halts
  via IntersectionObserver when off-screen, single static frame under reduced
  motion
- Tilt and magnetic effects are `pointerType === 'mouse'` only, so touch
  scrolling is never hijacked
- Every effect is inert under `prefers-reduced-motion`
- Footer is a server component and ships no JS

---

## [0.7.0] Page assembly

Canonical order, enforced in `app/page.tsx`:

1. Top disclosure bar — persistent, non-dismissable, inside the fixed chrome so
   it never scrolls away
2. Sticky header — logo, anchor nav, solid call button
3. Hero — eyebrow, H1, subline, `<PriceLockup />`, **ZIP checker *and* call
   button**, trust chips
4. Service lines via `activeServiceLines` → Fiber, Bundles, TV. Tone alternates
   light/dark automatically by index, so the rhythm survives adding or removing
   a line.
5. Honest fine-print grid (`#details`) + add-ons (`#addons`)
6. How it works / why us (`#why`), then service area (`#coverage`)
7. FAQ (`#faq`)
8. Footer (`#legal`)

`#coverage` is an addition, placed between "why us" and the FAQ. It does not
reorder any of the required sections.

**Footer** follows the structure of bestfiberinternet.us — four columns (Plans /
Details / Help / Talk to a human), retailer lockup and description, a disclosure
block, then a bottom bar of legal links and copyright — rendered dark. That
site's "Pricing current as of <date>" line and its "Retailer vs. ordering
direct" link were **not** carried over; both are forbidden here.

**ZIP checker** validates a 5-digit ZIP and hands off to a call. It does not
claim an availability result, because there is no lookup service behind it and
inventing one would be both false and a policy problem.

