## [1.0.0] Client revision — white theme, exact brand shades, copy pass

Requested changes after the first review of the build.

**Hero background animation removed.** The `FiberField` canvas is gone from the
hero and `components/visuals/FiberField.tsx` is deleted rather than orphaned.
The hero is now a plain white surface with no canvas, no grid overlay and no
parallax layer. The only motion left in it is the copy revealing on entry.

**How-it-works diagram removed.** `NetworkAssembly` is gone and its file is
deleted. "Three steps to installation" is now a clean three-column step list
with a rule above each step, so the section carries no scroll-scrubbed diagram,
no SVG path draw and no travelling pulse. The `.fiber-pulse` keyframe that fed
it was deleted from `globals.css` along with it.

Removing both cut the page bundle from 25.2 kB to 21.7 kB (143 kB to 139 kB
first load).

**White theme on exact Bluepeak shades.** Every dark section was converted to
white or `#f3f3ed`, and the palette now follows how mybluepeak.com actually uses
its own tokens rather than inverting them:

| Element | Shade | Token |
|---|---|---|
| Page and hero background | `#ffffff` | white |
| Alternating sections | `#f3f3ed` | light-gray |
| Headings, disclosure bar, footer | `#060048` | navy-blue |
| Featured plan card | `#0a109d` | dark-blue |
| All action buttons | `#008dbb` | teal |
| Badges, accent rule, step markers | `#ebab32` | yellow |

Confirmed in the browser by reading computed styles: every one of those resolves
to the exact hex above.

Card treatment now mirrors the live site — white cards across the row with the
featured plan inverted onto royal blue, a gold badge, a price-lock pill and the
same teal button on every card. A gold rule was added under the sticky header to
match the accent bar on mybluepeak.com.

Sections alternate white and `#f3f3ed`, and the clip-path wipe moved onto the
muted sections, where the angled top edge is visible against the white section
above it.

**Copy professionalised.** All headings rewritten, and the two section intros
that were called out with them:

| Before | After |
|---|---|
| Fiber internet built for your modern life. | Fiber internet engineered for the connected home. |
| Symmetrical fiber, priced in plain English | Fiber internet plans with transparent pricing |
| Put internet and TV on one bill | Internet and TV on one monthly bill |
| Live TV and your streaming apps, together | Live television and streaming in one experience |
| What it costs, line by line | Complete pricing and inclusions |
| Add-ons, if you want them | Optional service add-ons |
| Three steps, one phone call | Three steps to installation |
| Built on fiber, sold without the asterisks | A fiber network built for consistent performance |
| Where Bluepeak builds | Bluepeak service areas |

The FAQ heading went from "The things people ask before ordering" to
"Frequently asked questions" as well — not on the list, but it was the last
conversational heading left and would have clashed with the rest.

Section copy that was still hardcoded in `FinePrint.tsx` moved into
`finePrintCopy` and `addOnsCopy` in `lib/content.ts`, and the benefits block
gained `benefitsEyebrow` / `benefitsHeading` / `benefitsIntro`, so no heading is
stranded in a layout file any more.

**Service area rebuilt.** The chip-covered state cards are gone. It is now a
two-column section: a stat strip (states served, communities, FTTH) and a call
CTA on the left, and a single bordered directory on the right with one row per
state, a gold rule under each state name, and communities set as running text.
States sort by size, so the largest footprint leads instead of a one-city state.
On phones the stat strip becomes value/label rows, because three columns put the
labels on top of each other at 320px.

**Fixes made during this pass**
- A JSX comment placed as a sibling root inside a `.map()` callback is a syntax
  error; moved it above the callback.
- Stat labels collided at 320px; the strip now stacks, with `flex-row-reverse`
  and `sm:flex-col-reverse` keeping each value ahead of its label while `dt`
  still precedes `dd` in the markup.

**Tooling note:** running `next build` while `next dev` is live corrupts the
shared `.next` directory and takes the dev server down with webpack module
errors. Stop the dev server (or use a separate output dir) before building.

---

## [1.1.0] Client revision — gold speed gauge, hero, coverage

Second review raised four things: the hero lineup card was weak, the hero read
as generic, the coverage boxes still looked wrong, and the gold was barely
visible. One idea addresses all four.

**`SpeedGauge` (new).** Bluepeak's own plan cards use a half-round dial with a
gold arc that fills in proportion to the tier. Rebuilt here as
`components/visuals/SpeedGauge.tsx`: an SVG half-arc whose fill is
`speedMbps / topSpeedMbps`, so 1 Gig reads as a short sweep and 5 Gig fills the
dial. The arc draws once on entry and is static under reduced motion. This is
now the main carrier of `#ebab32` across the site.

The maximum is derived, not hardcoded — `topSpeedMbps` in `lib/content.ts` — so
adding a faster tier rescales every dial on the page automatically.

**Hero rebuilt.** The plain three-row lineup list is gone. The right column is
now a featured plan card on royal blue carrying the gold dial, a gold badge, the
price lockup and the top inclusions, with its own CTA. The left column gained a
clearer two-stat hierarchy — "Plans from $55.00/mo" against "Up to 5 Gig" — with
a rule separating the pitch from the ZIP form.

Which plan is featured comes from `featuredPlan` in `lib/content.ts` (the fiber
tier flagged `isPopular`, falling back to the lead plan), so moving the flag
moves the hero card.

**Gold spread across the page.** Beyond the dials: the tick that opens every
section eyebrow, the ticker bullets, the header rule, plan badges, the step
rules in "Three steps to installation", the stat markers and state markers in
the service area, and the separators between community names. Ten components
now carry it, against three before.

**Service area rebuilt again.** The first attempt failed for a structural
reason: the directory was squeezed into a `0.85fr / 1.15fr` split, so Oklahoma's
nineteen communities had roughly half the page to wrap into and looked cramped
however they were styled. It is now full-width — heading and CTA on one line, a
three-up stat band, then one full-width row per state with a gold marker, the
community count right-aligned, and the communities as running text underneath.

**Fixes made during this pass**
- The community list had no line-break opportunities. Each city sat in a
  `whitespace-nowrap` span with no whitespace between spans, so Oklahoma's row
  was one unbreakable 1,400px line that ran past the shell. The separators now
  carry real spaces (`{' · '}`) and the row wraps to two lines.
- The price lock was printed twice on every fiber card — once as a feature and
  once as the pill beneath. The list now filters out the entry matching
  `plan.priceLock`.
- The hero card had a "Most chosen plan" eyebrow directly above a "Most popular"
  badge. Dropped the eyebrow.

