## [1.2.0] Photography

Seven images delivered and wired in. They arrived as PNGs at 1408x768 rather
than the per-slot ratios in `IMAGE-BRIEF.md`, which is fine — each slot sets its
own aspect ratio and uses `object-position` to choose what survives the crop.

**Weight.** The PNGs totalled **47 MB**, all inside `public/`, which Next.js
deploys wholesale. Re-encoded to mozjpeg q82 they come to **808 KB** for all
seven, a 98% reduction with no visible loss. The originals were moved to
`_source-images/` (gitignored) so they stay on disk but never ship.

**Delivery.** Everything goes through `next/image`, so each photo is re-encoded
to AVIF at the size the device actually requests — the hero comes down to about
**27 KB at 1200px wide**. The hero image carries `priority`; every other image
lazy-loads. `next.config.mjs` sets AVIF/WebP and a one-year cache TTL.

**Placement**

| Image | Slot |
|---|---|
| `hero-connected-home` | Hero, right column, with the featured plan card overlapping its lower edge |
| `bundle-internet-and-tv` | Beside the "Internet and TV on one monthly bill" heading |
| `tv-evening-viewing` | Beside the "Live television and streaming" heading |
| `install-technician` | Two-up panel closing "Three steps to installation" |
| `wifi-equipment` | Beside the "A fiber network built for consistent performance" heading |
| `coverage-neighborhood` | Full-width 5:2 band in the service area |
| `og-share` | Open Graph and Twitter card metadata |

Paths and alt text live together in `images` in `lib/content.ts`, so a swapped
photo cannot keep a description that no longer matches it. `sectionImages` maps
service lines to photos, which is what makes `PlanSection` switch to its
two-column heading layout for bundles and TV while fiber stays text-only.

**Gemini watermark — cropped, on the owner's instruction.** Six of the seven
carried Google's visible sparkle in the bottom-right corner; `install-technician`
was clean. Flagged rather than actioned at first, because removing a provenance
mark is the owner's call. They asked for the crop.

**150px came off the right edge** of the six, taking them to 1258x768;
`install-technician` was left whole at 1408x768. Re-encoded from the original
PNGs rather than from the already-compressed JPEGs, so there is no second
generation loss. Every bottom-right corner was re-inspected afterwards to
confirm the sparkle was actually outside the new frame rather than assumed to
be. All seven are clean. Total on disk: **736 KB**.

Two knock-on corrections the reframe forced:

- The frames are now 1.638:1 rather than 1.833:1, so anything anchored by
  fraction shifted. `wifi-equipment` moved its router from roughly 66% of the
  frame to 74%, and its `objectPosition` went to 70% to follow it. That image is
  used as a full-bleed section background, where horizontal cropping actually
  happens, so it mattered.
- The Open Graph `width` in `app/layout.tsx` still declared 1408 and is now
  1258. A wrong declared size makes link previews letterbox.

**Watch out when replacing an image in place.** Next's image optimizer caches by
URL, and the URL does not change when the file behind it does — the dev server
kept serving the pre-crop frames, which showed up as a served aspect of 1.834
against a source of 1.638. Clearing `.next` fixes it. A production build is
unaffected, since it starts from an empty cache.

---

## [1.3.0] Photographic sections, hero rebuild, availability

**`PhotoBackdrop` (new).** Full-bleed photographic section background: the image
at `-z-20`, a navy scrim at `-z-10`, content above. The scrim is load-bearing,
not decoration — every one of these sections sets white text over a photograph.

Two modes. `left` weights the darkness to one side for the hero, where the copy
occupies one column and the photograph should stay visible in the other. `even`
washes the whole frame for sections whose content spans the full width. Both
fall back to an even wash below `lg`, because the left-weighted version only
makes sense once the copy has its own column — on a phone the content spans the
full width and would have run onto the bright side of the gradient.

**Hero rebuilt.** The previous version put a photo panel in the right column
with the plan card overlapping it, and the card was tall enough to cover most of
the photo — the reason it read as broken. Now the photograph is the hero's
full-bleed background, the copy sits on near-solid navy on the left, and the
plan card is a compact white card floating on the right: dial, name, price, CTA,
nothing else. The feature list moved out; it already appears on the plans below.

**Photographic sections.** `tv` and the "Why Bluepeak fiber" block now carry
their photograph as the whole section background. A service line can declare its
own treatment through `tone` on `serviceLineMeta` (`white` | `muted` | `photo`),
and anything that does not declare one falls back to the alternating white and
muted rhythm, so the page keeps working if a line is added or removed.

**Availability rebuilt.** Checked the two reference sites, and neither carries a
service-area directory at all: bestfiberinternet.us leads with "See what's
available at your address" plus a ZIP field and "Free to check · No obligation ·
Or call", and kineticfiber.us has no coverage section, routing everything to the
phone. Both are right — a visitor wants an answer about *their* address, not a
list of towns, which is why two attempts at presenting 31 communities as a
browsable list both read as filler.

The section is now the ZIP check, centred over the neighbourhood photograph,
with the footprint reduced to three stats and the communities kept as one quiet
line for local search.

**Copy**

| Before | After |
|---|---|
| Fiber internet engineered for the connected home. | High-speed fiber internet for the modern home. |
| Fiber internet plans with transparent pricing | Fiber internet plans and pricing |

**Fix during this pass.** The first `even` scrim was 86–94% opaque navy, which
buried the photograph so completely that the TV section rendered as a flat navy
block. Now 70–86% on desktop, 84–92% on mobile.

**Third scrim mode, `soft`.** Even at 70% the wash over "Why Bluepeak fiber"
was too heavy, because that photograph is a bright hallway and the white text
needed the cover. Rather than trade one against the other, the contrast moved
onto the content: the benefit cards became opaque (`bg-navy/80` with a 12px
backdrop blur), which freed the section scrim to drop to **82% at the top, 24%
through the middle** — the heading stays protected, and the photograph reads
everywhere else. That is the rule for `soft`: only use it where the content
below carries its own backing.

**Hero rebuilt again, as a split.** The full-bleed-with-scrim version was still
wrong, for three reasons that compounded: the scrim had to be heavy enough to
carry white text, which left the photograph as a dark blur; the plan card was
full height, which pushed the section past two screens; and the resulting shape
— big type left, floating card right — is the stock template.

Splitting the frame fixes all three at once. The photograph now runs full-bleed
down the right 46% **with no scrim over it at all**, so it is seen at full
strength, and the copy sits on solid navy where contrast was never in question.
A feathered gradient at the seam resolves one into the other. The plan moved
from a full-height card into a short horizontal bar — dial, name, price, CTA in
a row — which is what brings the hero back to a single screen: **956px against
a 900px viewport, down from over two screens.**

`SpeedGauge` gained a `compact` variant for that bar. The oversized "Plans from
/ Up to" number block became one dense line of text.

On small screens the split stacks: navy copy panel, then the photograph as a 5:3
band beneath it.

**Hero photograph corrected, and untinted.** Two problems, one of them a real
geometry mistake on my part. The source is 1.83:1 and the desktop column is
portrait, so only about a third of the frame's width survives the crop —
`objectPosition` at 58% put that third on the empty wall to the left of the
subjects rather than on the subjects themselves. It is now 70%, which holds both
figures in shot. The mobile band is 5:3, close to the source's own ratio, so it
needs far less repositioning and sits at 60%.

Every tint was removed: the `bg-navy/15` wash and the feathered seam gradient
are gone on desktop, and the top fade is gone on mobile. The split is a clean
edge and the photograph is shown exactly as delivered.

Because the seam is now hard, the copy column is capped at
`min(560px,46vw)` and the photograph narrowed to 42%, so text can never run
underneath it. Measured at both ends: 74px of clearance at 1024, 106px at 1340.

**Headline highlight.** "fiber internet" is picked out of the hero headline in
Bluepeak gold, tying the type back to the dials and badges. `WordReveal` gained
an optional `highlight` prop that locates the phrase within the split words and
colours only those, so the per-word mask animation is untouched. Matching
ignores punctuation and case, and a phrase that is not found is simply ignored
rather than throwing — the headline and the phrase both live in `lib/content.ts`
(`hero.headlineHighlight`), so they can drift apart when copy is edited.

**Featured plan highlighted.** The hero's plan bar is ringed in
`#ebab32` with a deeper drop shadow and a larger badge, so it reads as the pick
rather than as another white panel.

**Hero photograph made full-bleed, shading switched to neutral black.** The
split put the photograph on 42% of the frame; it now covers the whole hero, edge
to edge, and the separate mobile band is gone — one image element instead of
two.

The shading could not simply be deleted, and the reason is measurable rather
than a matter of taste. Sampling the source across five vertical bands gives
luminance **155, 120, 116, 100, 94** from left to right: its brightest region is
the window at the far left, which is exactly where the copy sits. White text on
that reads at roughly **2.8:1** and fails WCAG AA.

So the shading stayed but lost its hue. It is now pure `rgba(0,0,0,…)` with no
blue component at all, weighted left on desktop and even below `lg`. The
photograph darkens without tinting and keeps its own colour — the warm greys and
skin tones stay warm, which is what the navy scrim had been destroying. Over the
bright left band the composite lands near luminance 19, about **17:1** against
white.

If the photograph is ever regenerated, one with a darker or emptier left third
would allow the shading to come down further.

**Hero call CTA removed.** The duplicate "Call to order" button and the
"Speak to a person now" hours line are gone from the hero's left column. The
original brief required the hero to hold both a ZIP checker and a call button,
and it still does — the featured plan card in the hero carries a "Call to order"
CTA, verified in the rendered DOM.

**Known limit.** The source images are 1408x768. Used as full-bleed backgrounds
they upscale on displays wider than about 1400px. The scrim hides it, but larger
sources would be better if these are ever regenerated.

---

## [1.4.0] Ambient motion

Two additions, both deliberately cheap. The earlier hero canvas and the
scroll-scrubbed diagram were removed for being heavy and gimmicky, so neither of
these introduces a canvas, a timer or any per-frame JavaScript.

**Hero — `HeroBackdrop` (new).** Two layers of motion on the photograph, and
neither touches its colour:

- a slow Ken Burns push-in (`scale(1.06)` to `scale(1.16)` with a small drift)
  running as a CSS animation, 26s, alternating;
- a scroll parallax, so the frame travels 14% slower than the copy over it.

They sit on **separate elements on purpose**. Both animate `transform`, and one
would silently overwrite the other on a shared node. The push-in starts already
scaled so the frame never exposes an edge as it drifts.

**Light sections — `AmbientShades` (new).** Three heavily blurred brand-tinted
discs — teal, royal, gold — drifting behind the content on 30s, 38s and 34s
cycles. Applied to the five light sections: fiber, bundles, the fine-print grid,
"how it works" and the FAQ. The photographic sections are left alone, since they
already carry an image.

Only `transform` animates, so this stays on the compositor and never triggers
layout or paint. Opacity is held low (0.14–0.25 before blur) so it reads as tint
in the paper rather than as decoration competing with the cards.

Stacked by document order rather than negative z-index: the parent clips with
`overflow-hidden` and the section's own content follows in a `relative` wrapper.
A negative z-index would have painted the discs behind the section's own
background colour and made them invisible — the same trap the first `PhotoBackdrop`
scrim fell into from the other direction.

**Verified in the browser rather than assumed:** `ken-burns / 26s` on the hero
image, `drift-a / 30s` and `drift-b / 38s` on the shades, five sections carrying
them, every disc clipped by an `overflow-hidden` ancestor, and 17 animations
running. All four keyframes are present in the production CSS.

Horizontal overflow at 320px re-checked afterwards: still none. The discs are
wider than their sections by design and show up in an element-by-element scan,
exactly as the marquee track does; `scrollWidth === clientWidth === 320` is the
check that matters.

---

## Verification results

- `tsc --noEmit` — clean
- `next build` — succeeds, route `/` prerendered static, **146 kB** first-load JS
- Images: 808 kB on disk for seven photos, served as AVIF (hero ~27 kB at 1200px)
- Horizontal overflow at 320px — none (`scrollWidth === clientWidth === 320`),
  with zero offending elements outside the clipped marquee track
- Brand shades verified from computed styles: `#ffffff`, `#f3f3ed`, `#060048`,
  `#0a109d`, `#008dbb`, `#ebab32`
- Hero contains no `<canvas>`; no install diagram remains in the DOM
- Every `tel:` link carries `data-call-cta`
- Cascade verified: changing a price in `lib/content.ts` updates the hero, the
  cards, the comparison table and the footer disclosure together

